Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>12  Files</H1>
<HR>
So far, everything your programs have made has vanished when they stopped. In this chapter you will learn
to read and write files: Doodle will save its drawings and load them again, remember your favourite colour,
and you'll write a program that counts the words in any text file dropped on it.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Files and their names</H2>
Every file on a RISC OS disc has a full name, or <I>pathname</I>, that says exactly where it is: which
filing system and disc, then each directory from the root directory <CODE>$</CODE> down, separated by
full stops. The Hello program from chapter 1 is<P>
<PRE>
ADFS::HardDisc4.$.MyProgs.Hello
</PRE>
<CODE>ADFS</CODE> is the filing system, <CODE>HardDisc4</CODE> the disc, and <CODE>MyProgs</CODE> the directory.
The title of a directory display always shows its full name.<P>
Every file also has a <I>file type</I>, a number from 0 to 4095 usually written in hexadecimal (base 16)
with an <CODE>&amp;</CODE> in front: Text is &amp;FFF, BASIC is &amp;FFB, JSScript is &amp;F81. In JavaScript a
hexadecimal number is written with <CODE>0x</CODE> in front instead, so Text is <CODE>0xfff</CODE>. Appendix E
of the User Guide lists the file types.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Reading and writing</H2>
Your programs reach the desktop's discs through <CODE>vfs</CODE> (the filing system), which is ready to use in
a script and imported from <CODE>'riscos'</CODE> in a module. It has functions to write, read and look for
files:<P>
<DL>
<DT><CODE>vfs.writeFile(path, text, { filetype: 0xfff })</CODE>
<DD>writes <CODE>text</CODE> to the file <CODE>path</CODE>, giving it the file type, and makes the file if it
doesn't exist. Anything that was in the file before is replaced.<P>
<DT><CODE>await vfs.readText(path)</CODE>
<DD>reads a file and gives back its contents as a string. Reading from a disc can take a moment, so, as with
<CODE>input</CODE>, put <CODE>await</CODE> in front.<P>
<DT><CODE>vfs.exists(path)</CODE>
<DD>is <CODE>true</CODE> if there is a file or directory called <CODE>path</CODE>.<P>
<DT><CODE>vfs.list(path)</CODE>
<DD>gives an array with an object for each file in the directory <CODE>path</CODE>. Each object has the file's
<CODE>name</CODE>, its <CODE>size</CODE> in bytes, its <CODE>filetype</CODE>, and <CODE>type</CODE>, which is
<CODE>'file'</CODE> or <CODE>'dir'</CODE>.<P>
</DL>
If something goes wrong - reading a file that isn't there, say - you get an error with the reason, such as
<I>File 'Nothing' not found</I>.<P>

<H3>Where am I?</H3>
A program often needs files that are kept beside it. It can't know in advance where you will put it, so
<CODE>ctx</CODE> tells it: <CODE>ctx.file</CODE> is the full name of the program itself, and
<CODE>ctx.dir</CODE> the directory it is in. This short script lists the files in its own directory:<P>
<PRE>
for (const f of vfs.list(ctx.dir)) {
  print(f.name, f.size);
}
</PRE>
Try it: save it as a JSScript file in MyProgs and run it.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Data as text: JSON</H2>
Files hold text (or bytes), but Doodle's drawing is an array of objects, each holding a colour and another
array of objects. To save it, it has to be turned into text, in a way that can be turned back again.<P>
JavaScript has a standard way to do that, called <I>JSON</I> (JavaScript Object Notation):<P>
<DL>
<DT><CODE>JSON.stringify(value)</CODE>
<DD>turns numbers, strings, <CODE>true</CODE> and <CODE>false</CODE>, arrays and objects - and arrays of
objects of arrays, and so on - into a string.<P>
<DT><CODE>JSON.parse(text)</CODE>
<DD>turns such a string back into the value it came from.<P>
</DL>
For example, a drawing of one short red line becomes this text, which looks very like JavaScript:<P>
<PRE>
[{"colour":"#ff0000","points":[{"x":1,"y":2},{"x":8,"y":9}]}]
</PRE>
If the text isn't JSON, <CODE>JSON.parse</CODE> gives an error saying what it didn't expect, and where.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Doodle4: saving and loading</H2>
Doodle4 is Doodle3 with a Save box on its menu. It saves a drawing as a Text file holding JSON, and loads
one when you drop it on its window or on its icon bar icon. Like Doodle3 it is a directory with Main and
Picture modules; run it by double-clicking Main.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Doodle4Save" WIDTH=614 HEIGHT=461></CENTER>
Draw something, move to Save on the menu and drag the file icon from the Save box into a directory display.
Then choose Clear, and drag the file you saved back onto the Doodle4 window: the drawing comes back, and the
window's title shows the file's name.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Doodle4" WIDTH=490 HEIGHT=389></CENTER>
Picture has two new functions at the end:<P>
<PRE>
// the picture as text, for saving in a file
export function pictureToText() {
  return JSON.stringify(lines);
}

// replace the picture with one saved by pictureToText
export function pictureFromText(text) {
  const saved = JSON.parse(text);
  if (!Array.isArray(saved)) {
    throw new Error('This is not a drawing');
  }
  lines.length = 0;
  for (const line of saved) {
    lines.push(line);
  }
  drawing = null;
}
</PRE>
<CODE>pictureToText</CODE> is just <CODE>JSON.stringify</CODE>. <CODE>pictureFromText</CODE> parses the text,
checks that it really is an array with <CODE>Array.isArray</CODE>, and puts its lines into
<CODE>lines</CODE> in place of the old ones.<P>
Here is the whole of Main:<P>
<PRE>
// Doodle4 - Doodle3 that saves and loads its drawings
import {
  Menu, colourMenu, infoBox, saveAs, wimpColour, wimp, vfs,
} from 'riscos';
import {
  startLine, addPoint, endLine, clear, isEmpty, drawPicture,
  pictureToText, pictureFromText,
} from './Picture';

export default async function start(task, ctx) {
  // the colour used last time is kept in a file next to the program
  const choices = ctx.dir + '.Choices';
  let colour = 7;
  if (vfs.exists(choices)) {
    colour = Number(await vfs.readText(choices));
  }
  let filename = 'Drawing';   // where the drawing was saved or loaded

  const w = task.createWindow({
    title: filename,
    x: 160, y: 100, w: 480, h: 360,
    extent: { w: 480, h: 360 },
    flags: { title: true, close: true, back: true, moveable: true },
    colours: { workBg: 0 },
    workButton: 'click',
  });
  w.useCanvas((g) =&gt; drawPicture(g));

  function setColour(n) {
    colour = n;
    vfs.writeFile(choices, String(n), { filetype: 0xfff });
  }

  function saveBox() {
    return saveAs({
      task, filename, filetype: 0xfff,
      getData: () =&gt; pictureToText(),
      onSaved: (path) =&gt; {
        if (!path) return;       // saved into another application
        filename = path;
        w.setTitle(filename);
      },
    });
  }

  // load a drawing from a file dropped on the window or the icon
  async function load(file) {
    if (file.filetype !== 0xfff) {
      task.reportError(`'${file.name}' is not a text file`);
      return;
    }
    try {
      pictureFromText(await vfs.readText(file.path));
    } catch (e) {
      task.reportError(`'${file.name}' is not a Doodle drawing`);
      return;
    }
    filename = file.path;
    w.setTitle(filename);
    w.open({ behind: 'top' });
    w.invalidate();
  }

  const info = infoBox(task, {
    name: 'Doodle4', purpose: 'Draws doodles',
    author: 'A. Programmer', version: '1.00',
  });
  function doodleMenu() {
    return new Menu('Doodle4', [
      { text: 'Info', submenu: info },
      { text: 'Save', submenu: saveBox },
      { text: 'Colour',
        submenu: colourMenu('Colour', colour, (n) =&gt; setColour(n)) },
      { text: 'Clear', shaded: isEmpty(), dotted: true,
        action: () =&gt; {
          clear();
          w.invalidate();
        } },
      { text: 'Quit', action: () =&gt; task.quit() },
    ]);
  }

  w.on('click', (ev) =&gt; {
    if (ev.button === 'select') {
      startLine(wimpColour(colour), ev.x, ev.y);
      w.invalidate();
    } else if (ev.button === 'menu') {
      wimp.menus.openAt(doodleMenu(), ev, { task });
    }
    return true;
  });

  w.on('pointermove', (ev) =&gt; {
    if (ev.buttons === 1) {
      if (addPoint(ev.x, ev.y)) w.invalidate();
    } else {
      endLine();
    }
  });

  w.on('dataload', (ev) =&gt; {
    load(ev.files[0]);
    return true;
  });

  task.addIconbarIcon({
    sprite: 'file_aff',
    onClick: () =&gt; w.open({ behind: 'top' }),
    menu: () =&gt; doodleMenu(),
    onDataLoad: (ev) =&gt; load(ev.files[0]),
  });
  task.onMessage('Quit', () =&gt; task.quit());

  w.open();
}
</PRE>

<H3>The Save box</H3>
<CODE>saveAs</CODE> makes the standard RISC OS Save box, with a file icon, a name and an OK button:<P>
<PRE>
  function saveBox() {
    return saveAs({
      task, filename, filetype: 0xfff,
      getData: () =&gt; pictureToText(),
      onSaved: (path) =&gt; {
        if (!path) return;       // saved into another application
        filename = path;
        w.setTitle(filename);
      },
    });
  }
</PRE>
<DL>
<DT><CODE>filename</CODE>
<DD>the name to show in the box.<P>
<DT><CODE>filetype</CODE>
<DD>the type of the file to save; the box shows its icon.<P>
<DT><CODE>getData</CODE>
<DD>a function that gives the text to save. It is called only when the user actually saves.<P>
<DT><CODE>onSaved</CODE>
<DD>a function called afterwards with the file's full name - or with <CODE>null</CODE> if the icon was
dropped into another application instead of a directory, as there is then no file.<P>
</DL>
The box does all the rest itself: it lets the user drag the icon to a directory display, or type a full
name and click OK, and it writes the file with <CODE>vfs.writeFile</CODE>. Doodle4 remembers the name in
<CODE>onSaved</CODE> (unless it is <CODE>null</CODE>) and puts it in the window's title, and the next Save box starts with it, so saving again
is just a click on OK - as in Edit.<P>
The menu item is <CODE>{ text: 'Save', submenu: saveBox }</CODE>: the <I>function</I>
<CODE>saveBox</CODE>, not a box. A menu calls a submenu function when the submenu is opened, so each Save box
is made fresh, with the current name in it.<P>

<H3>Loading a dropped file</H3>
When a file is dropped on a window, the window gets a <CODE>dataload</CODE> event. <CODE>ev.files</CODE> is an
array of the files dropped, each an object with the file's <CODE>path</CODE> (full name), <CODE>name</CODE>
and <CODE>filetype</CODE>. Doodle4 loads the first:<P>
<PRE>
  w.on('dataload', (ev) =&gt; {
    load(ev.files[0]);
    return true;
  });
</PRE>
Returning <CODE>true</CODE> tells the desktop the drop has been dealt with. Files dropped on the icon bar icon
arrive in the same form, at the icon's <CODE>onDataLoad</CODE> function.<P>
Both call <CODE>load</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
  // load a drawing from a file dropped on the window or the icon
  async function load(file) {
    if (file.filetype !== 0xfff) {
      task.reportError(`'${file.name}' is not a text file`);
      return;
    }
    try {
      pictureFromText(await vfs.readText(file.path));
    } catch (e) {
      task.reportError(`'${file.name}' is not a Doodle drawing`);
      return;
    }
    filename = file.path;
    w.setTitle(filename);
    w.open({ behind: 'top' });
    w.invalidate();
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>load</CODE> is an <CODE>async</CODE> function: the word <CODE>async</CODE> in front of
<CODE>function</CODE> is what allows <CODE>await</CODE> inside it. (A script's top level is allowed to
<CODE>await</CODE> without it; a function has to say so.)<P>

<H3>When things go wrong: try and catch</H3>
People drop all sorts of files on programs, and a Text file that isn't a drawing would make
<CODE>JSON.parse</CODE> fail. Normally an error stops what the program was doing and shows an error box. To
deal with an error yourself, put the code that might fail inside <CODE>try { ... }</CODE>, followed by
<CODE>catch (e) { ... }</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
try {
  pictureFromText(await vfs.readText(file.path));
} catch (e) {
  task.reportError(`'${file.name}' is not a Doodle drawing`);
  return;
}
</PRE>
If anything in the <CODE>try</CODE> part goes wrong, JavaScript jumps straight to the <CODE>catch</CODE> part,
with the error in <CODE>e</CODE> (<CODE>e.message</CODE> is its message). If nothing goes wrong, the
<CODE>catch</CODE> part is skipped. Here the program reports a friendlier message, with
<CODE>task.reportError</CODE>, in the usual error box.<P>
You can make errors happen too: <CODE>throw new Error('message')</CODE>, as <CODE>pictureFromText</CODE> does
when the JSON isn't an array, stops the function and jumps to the nearest <CODE>catch</CODE>.<P>

<H3>Remembering a choice</H3>
Doodle4 also remembers the colour you last chose, in a small Text file called Choices kept beside the
program:<P>
<PRE>
export default async function start(task, ctx) {
  // the colour used last time is kept in a file next to the program
  const choices = ctx.dir + '.Choices';
  let colour = 7;
  if (vfs.exists(choices)) {
    colour = Number(await vfs.readText(choices));
  }
</PRE>
When it starts, it reads the file if there is one, and <CODE>Number</CODE> turns the text back into a
number. Each time a colour is chosen, <CODE>setColour</CODE> writes the new number to the file. Because it
awaits <CODE>readText</CODE>, <CODE>start</CODE> is <CODE>async</CODE> too, as the desktop's own applications'
start functions are.<P>
<B>Note</B>: a real RISC OS application would keep its choices in the <CODE>Choices:</CODE> directory in
<CODE>!Boot</CODE>, rather than beside itself, so that several people could share one copy of the program.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>WordCount</H2>
The last program in this chapter is a short script: drop a text file on its window and it counts the
lines, words and characters in it.<P>
<PRE>
// WordCount - drop a text file on the window to count its words
const w = task.createWindow({
  title: 'WordCount',
  x: 300, y: 200, w: 320, h: 172,
  extent: { w: 320, h: 172 },
  flags: { title: true, close: true, back: true, moveable: true },
  icons: [
    { x: 16, y: 12, w: 288, h: 32, text: 'Drop a text file here',
      hcentre: true },
    { x: 16, y: 56, w: 140, h: 28, text: 'Lines' },
    { x: 16, y: 92, w: 140, h: 28, text: 'Words' },
    { x: 16, y: 128, w: 140, h: 28, text: 'Characters' },
    { x: 170, y: 56, w: 134, h: 28, text: '-', rjustify: true },
    { x: 170, y: 92, w: 134, h: 28, text: '-', rjustify: true },
    { x: 170, y: 128, w: 134, h: 28, text: '-', rjustify: true },
  ],
});

const title = w.icons[0];
const lines = w.icons[4];
const words = w.icons[5];
const chars = w.icons[6];

async function count(file) {
  if (file.filetype !== 0xfff) {
    task.reportError(`'${file.name}' is not a text file`);
    return;
  }
  const text = await vfs.readText(file.path);
  // split at line ends, and at any run of spaces or line ends
  let lineCount = text.split('\n').length;
  if (text.endsWith('\n')) {
    lineCount = lineCount - 1;   // nothing after the last line end
  }
  const wordList = text.split(/\s+/).filter((s) =&gt; s !== '');
  title.setText(file.name);
  lines.setText(String(lineCount));
  words.setText(String(wordList.length));
  chars.setText(String(text.length));
}

w.on('dataload', (ev) =&gt; {
  count(ev.files[0]);
  return true;
});
w.on('close', () =&gt; task.quit());
w.open();
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/WordCount" WIDTH=330 HEIGHT=201></CENTER>
It uses the window icons of chapter 6, and the <CODE>dataload</CODE> event, as Doodle4 does. The counting is
done by <CODE>count</CODE>, which has a few new things:<P>
<UL>
<LI><CODE>text.endsWith('\n')</CODE> is <CODE>true</CODE> if the text ends with a line end
(<CODE>'\n'</CODE> is the character that ends a line). Most text files end with one, and there's no line
after it to count.<P>
<LI><CODE>/\s+/</CODE> is a <I>regular expression</I>, a pattern to match text. <CODE>\s</CODE> means any
space, tab or line end and <CODE>+</CODE> means "one or more", so <CODE>split(/\s+/)</CODE> splits the text
wherever there is any run of space between words - not just at single spaces, as <CODE>split(' ')</CODE> did
in chapter 5.<P>
<LI><CODE>filter</CODE> makes a new array of just the items for which a function gives <CODE>true</CODE>. Here
it drops any empty strings (from space at the very start or end of the text).<P>
<LI><CODE>String(n)</CODE> turns a number into a string, for <CODE>setText</CODE>.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Files have full names like <CODE>ADFS::HardDisc4.$.MyProgs.Hello</CODE>, and file types like &amp;FFF,
written <CODE>0xfff</CODE> in JavaScript.<P>
<LI><CODE>vfs.writeFile(path, text, { filetype })</CODE> writes a file; <CODE>await vfs.readText(path)</CODE>
reads one; <CODE>vfs.exists</CODE> and <CODE>vfs.list</CODE> look for files.<P>
<LI><CODE>ctx.dir</CODE> is the directory a program is in.<P>
<LI><CODE>JSON.stringify</CODE> turns data into text and <CODE>JSON.parse</CODE> turns it back.<P>
<LI><CODE>saveAs({ task, filename, filetype, getData, onSaved })</CODE> makes a Save box.<P>
<LI>Files dropped on a window arrive in its <CODE>dataload</CODE> event, as <CODE>ev.files</CODE>; files dropped
on an icon bar icon, at its <CODE>onDataLoad</CODE>.<P>
<LI><CODE>try { ... } catch (e) { ... }</CODE> deals with errors; <CODE>throw new Error(...)</CODE> makes one.
<CODE>await</CODE> can only be used in an <CODE>async</CODE> function (or at the top level of a
script).<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Write a script that asks for some text with <CODE>input</CODE> and appends it, as a new line, to a
Text file called Diary beside the program, so the file grows each time the program is run. (Read the file
first if it exists.)<P>
<DT><DD>2  Make WordCount also show the longest word in the file.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Add a <I>Save</I> key to Doodle4: pressing F3 while its window has the input focus should open the
Save box in the middle of the screen. (Use <CODE>wimp.setCaret(w)</CODE> when the window is clicked, as in
chapter 8, so that it gets keys; F3 has the key code <CODE>0x183</CODE>; and a Save box made by
<CODE>saveAs</CODE> can be opened on its own with <CODE>box.openCentred()</CODE>.)<P>
<DT><DD>4  Doodle4 saves its drawings as Text files, so double-clicking one opens it in Edit. Try it, and
look at the JSON. What happens if you change one of the numbers in Edit, save it, and drop the file on
Doodle4? What if you delete a bracket?<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  <PRE>
const diary = ctx.dir + '.Diary';
let text = '';
if (vfs.exists(diary)) {
  text = await vfs.readText(diary);
}
const entry = await input('What happened today? ');
vfs.writeFile(diary, text + entry + '\n', { filetype: 0xfff });
</PRE>
<DT><DD>2  Add a label and a value icon to the window (and make it taller), and in <CODE>count</CODE>:
<PRE>
let longest = '';
for (const word of wordList) {
  if (word.length &gt; longest.length) {
    longest = word;
  }
}
</PRE>
then show <CODE>longest</CODE> with <CODE>setText</CODE>.<P>
<DT><DD>3  In the click handler, add <CODE>wimp.setCaret(w);</CODE> to the Select branch, and add:
<PRE>
w.on('key', (ev) =&gt; {
  if (ev.code === 0x183) {
    saveBox().openCentred();
    return true;
  }
});
</PRE>
<DT><DD>4  A changed number moves that point of the line when the drawing is loaded. Deleting a bracket makes
the text no longer JSON, so <CODE>JSON.parse</CODE> fails and Doodle4 reports that the file is not a Doodle
drawing, thanks to <CODE>try</CODE> and <CODE>catch</CODE>.<P>
</DL>

